<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dayak people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dayaks, Dyaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dayak are the indigenous, mainly non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malay peoples</a> of the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, comprising many groups such as the Iban, Ngaju, Kayan and Kenyah. Traditionally they lived in longhouse communities along the rivers, practising shifting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation and, in some groups, headhunting. Coastal Malay sultanates such as Brunei, Sambas and Banjar drew tribute and forest produce from them, and many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 19th and 20th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dayak-people.jpg" alt="Dayak people" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Studioportret_van_een_Dajak_in_krijgskleding_TMnr_60033041.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society was hierarchical: the sultan and the pengiran nobility stood above the commoners of the capital, many of them traders, fishermen and craftsmen, while subject peoples of the interior, including Kedayan farmers and various <b>Dayak</b> groups, paid tribute in produce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans ruled a Malay Muslim riverine elite that collected tribute from inland <b>Dayak</b> communities, trading forest goods such as rattan, resin, gold and birds nests to Banjarmasin and Java.</blockquote><a href="/state/kotawaringin-sultanate">Kotawaringin Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brookes ruled personally, with a small European staff and Malay chiefs, and governed through longhouse headmen among Iban and other <b>Dayak peoples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Raj of Sarawak</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans ruled coastal Malay communities and drew tribute from <b>Dayak</b> villages inland.</blockquote><a href="/state/sambas-sultanate">Sambas Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
